prvUnlockQueue's Tx loop does not break out when the receive list is empty.

prvUnlockQueue (0x9e66c) walks cTxLock down and, for a queue that belongs to no
queue set, tests xTasksWaitingToReceive. The release breaks out of the loop when
that list is empty: `ldr r3, [r4, #0x24]; cbnz r3, <remove>` falls through to the
loop exit. The image tests the same word and branches the other way -- `ldr r3,
[r4, #0x24]; cmp r3, #0; beq <decrement>` at 0x9e6c8 -- so an empty list costs
one more turn of the loop instead of ending it. Those two extra bytes are why
the image's body is 124 where the release's is 122.

The Rx loop below it keeps its break, in both. That asymmetry is what names the
change as a source edit: one compiler and one set of flags cannot turn the break
into a continue in one loop of a function and leave the other loop alone, and
the block layout follows from it. With the break gone the loop body no longer
exits, so GCC puts it out of line behind a `bgt` at 0x9e680 and keeps the lock
store inline, which is the image's order and the other 68 differing bytes.

Both sides sibling-call vPortExitCritical at the end (`pop.w {r4, r5, r6, lr}`
then `b.w`); the earlier reading that only the image did was an artefact of
comparing the two disassemblies by instruction index rather than by address.

Measured: with the break removed the 124 bytes of prvUnlockQueue agree with the
image once the relocated fields are masked (abi/body_check.py).

--- a/queue.c
+++ b/queue.c
@@ -2216,7 +2216,7 @@
 					}
 					else
 					{
-						break;
+						mtCOVERAGE_TEST_MARKER();
 					}
 				}
 			}
